The concrete addition — extend the existing command, don't create a new one:


pub struct Watershed {
    pub maximum_finder_tolerance: f32,
    pub smoothing_sigma: f32,
    pub min_object_size: u32,
    /// NEW: what surface local maxima are seeded from. `DistanceMap` is
    /// today's only behavior. `Intensity` finds seeds on the (optionally
    /// smoothed) grayscale image instead, restricted to the foreground
    /// mask - the fix for diffusely-connected regions whose *shape* has
    /// no separate peaks but whose *brightness* clearly does.
    pub seed_source: SeedSource,
}

pub enum SeedSource {
    DistanceMap,
    Intensity,
}
maximum_finder_tolerance reuses cleanly either way — prominence/tolerance is the same concept whether it's applied to a distance map or a brightness map, just a different input surface. Flooding itself can stay exactly as implemented today (distance-transform-based), since that already matches this pipeline's Shape dividing-line setting — the only missing piece is the seed source.

This clears the "earns its place independently" bar easily: intensity-seeded/shape-flooded watershed (often called marker-controlled or H-maxima watershed) is the standard technique for splitting touching cells/nuclei in fluorescence microscopy generally, not a CometAssay-specific hack — CP's own IdentifyPrimaryObjects defaults to Intensity unclumping precisely because it's the common case. This is a bigger, more broadly useful gap than the declumping difference flagged for ExampleColocalization, and it's the one actually blocking a real result here (CometTails currently detects 0 objects, not just "close but off").